import React from "react";

const Theorytext = () => {
  return (
    <div
      className="p-4 scrollbar-primary"
      style={{ overflow: "auto", width: "100%" }}
    >
      <div>
        <strong>Objective</strong>
      </div>
      <div>To use the Wh-words in the sentence.</div>
      <br></br>
      <div><strong>Wh-interrogative</strong></div>
      <div>
        Wh-questions are interrogative questions that begin with one of the 'wh-' words.
      </div>
      <div>
        Wh-questions are a type of question that starts with wh-words. Questions
        are used to gather information about a topic or situation. You cannot
        answer wh-questions with 'yes' or 'no'.
      </div>
      <br></br>
      <ol>
        <strong><li>Who</li></strong>
        <div>
          “Who “refers to a person, and because it is a pronoun, it doesn’t come
          before a noun. When “who” is the object, we can instead use whom;
          however, the “who” question word is quite common in everyday
          conversation.
        </div>
        <div>E.g.: Who is this?</div>
        <br></br>
        <strong><li>Where</li></strong>
        <div>
          To get information regarding the location or position, we use “where”
          as an adverbial that may be the object of a preposition.
        </div>
        <div>E.g:, Where are we going?</div>
        <br></br>
        <strong><li>When</li></strong>
        <div>“When” is used to determine the occasion, time, etc.</div>
        <div>E.g.: When will the event begin?</div>
        <br></br>
        <strong><li>Why</li></strong>
        <div>
          “Why “is used to understand the reason or seek an explanation;
          however, “why don’t “is used to provide a recommendation.
        </div>
        <div>E.g.: Why are you shouting?</div>
        <br></br>
        <strong><li>What</li></strong>
        <div>
          “What” is a pronoun, which can be a subject or object. We need to
          understand that there will be no inversion of the subject and verb
          when the question words are the subject.
        </div>
        <div>E.g.: What is the time?</div>
        <br></br>
        <strong><li>Which</li></strong>
        <div>“Which” can be used as a pronoun and determiner.</div>
        <div>E.g.: Which is your favourite colour?</div>
        <div>They are also known as question words.</div>
        <br></br>
        <strong><li>Whose</li></strong>
        <div>
          “Whose” refers to the pronoun possessive form, i.e., “who”. It refers
          to a person.
        </div>
        <div>E.g.: Whose mobile is this?</div>
        <br></br>
        <strong><li>How</li></strong>
        <div>
          “How” is used to ask both direct and indirect questions to obtain
          valuable knowledge regarding the amount of distance for expressing the
          degree level, and it refers to a method or way.
        </div>
        <div>E.g:, How was the match?</div>
      </ol>
    </div>
  );
};

export default Theorytext;
